# Line endings, decided here rather than by whatever core.autocrlf each machine
# happens to have. Development is on Windows; the site runs on Linux.

# Shell scripts MUST be LF. A Bash script checked out with CRLF fails on Linux
# with "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M" -- and docs/deploy.sh is run on the server.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Git hooks have no extension, so *.sh misses them. Same requirement: a hook
# checked out with CRLF fails the moment anyone runs it on Linux or macOS.
docs/hooks/* text eol=lf

# Databases are binary. Git detects this on its own today, but a misconfigured
# core.autocrlf rewriting bytes inside Track.db would corrupt it silently, and
# that file is the source of everything the site serves.
*.db binary

# The precomputed artifacts are written compact -- single-line, no newlines to
# convert -- but marking them keeps that true if one is ever pretty-printed, and
# keeps their bytes identical on both platforms. The routes send these files
# verbatim, so their bytes are the response.
*.json text eol=lf

# Images and fonts.
*.webp binary
*.png binary
*.pdf binary
